f(7,'workedExample','On the stated Linux ABI a null pointer uses address zero. If generated code accesses that unmapped address, hardware faults and Linux normally delivers SIGSEGV. The C language itself makes null-pointer dereference undefined behavior. vm.mmap_min_addr restricts low mappings subject to configuration, capabilities and security policy; privileged exceptions exist. Low-mapping restrictions also mitigate some kernel null-dereference exploits.','No universal NULLrepresentation ornevermap.',vm)
f(8,'workedExample','8086 real mode computed segment×16+offset with 20-bit addressing. The 286 added protected segmentation; the 386 added paging. Later 32-bit Linux largely used flat code/data segments, with exceptions. Native 64-bit mode ignores CS/DS/ES/SS bases and ordinary segment limits, while FS/GS bases remain useful for TLS and per-CPU data. Compatibility mode has different segmentation semantics.','Historical/longmodescope.',fs)
